# SRG-APP-000375-AU-000280  -  CAT III
# The Central Log Server must be configured to record time stamps for when
# log records are received by the log server that meet a granularity of one
# second for a minimum degree of precision.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000375-AU-000280
group:           V-206505
severity:        low
cat:             CAT III
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-001889
control_rev5:    AU-8

contribution:    Splunk stores event time as an epoch value with a subsecond extension,
                 which already exceeds a one-second floor; the application's configurable
                 share is whether the strptime format applied to a source type reads the
                 record's seconds field rather than truncating to a coarser unit.

governs:         props.conf - How incoming data is recognized, parsed and timestamped.

check:           props.conf [<source type>] TIME_FORMAT matches %S

external:        Whether the sending host emits a timestamp carrying seconds at all is a
                 property of that host's own logging configuration, not of Splunk.
external:        Host clock resolution and synchronization are operating system and time
                 service functions.

citation:        props.conf.spec:554 TIME_FORMAT = <strptime-style format> and :562-564
                 "the <strptime-style format> should describe the day of the year and the
                 time of day"; props.conf.spec:684-700 ADD_EXTRA_TIME_FIELDS = [none |
                 subseconds | all | <boolean>], default true. No condition is written over
                 ADD_EXTRA_TIME_FIELDS: the specification states that setting it to "none"
                 strips the index-time timestamp fields but still returns the
                 second-granularity timestamp in _time, which meets this requirement's
                 one-second minimum, so a finding on that value would be a false finding.
finding:         Assessed at the application layer alone: a TIME_FORMAT set for an audit
                 source type reads no seconds field, so the recorded receipt timestamp for
                 those records is coarser than the one-second minimum.
